  "theory": {
        "intro": "Factorising runs the special products backwards. Three shapes are worth recognising on sight: a **difference of two squares**, a **perfect square trinomial**, and a **four-term expression** that groups in pairs.",
        "facts": [
          "**Difference of two squares:** $a^2-b^2=(a-b)(a+b)$.",
          "A **sum** of two squares such as $x^2+9$ does not factorise.",
          "**Perfect square trinomial:** $a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2$ and $a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2$.",
          "Check the middle term really is $2ab$ before using the perfect square rule.",
          "**Take out any common factor first**: $2x^2-72 = 2(x^2-36) = 2(x-6)(x+6)$.",
          "Square-root the coefficient as well as the variable: $\\sqrt{25x^2}=5x$, $\\sqrt{x^6}=x^3$ and $\\sqrt{16y^2}=4y$.",
          "**Grouping in pairs** handles four terms: factorise each pair, then take out the common binomial factor.",
          "Take out a negative factor when that is what makes the two binomials match.",
          "A **non-monic** trinomial $ax^2+bx+c$ splits: find two numbers with product $ac$ and sum $b$, split $bx$, then group in pairs.",
          "An answer is **fully factorised** when no bracket still contains a common factor or another difference of two squares."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Take out any common factor.",
          "Identify which form the expression matches.",
          "Apply the matching factorisation.",
          "Check by expanding."
        ]
      },
